# LINEAR MAPS AS A CATEGORY — graphical linear algebra, level 1.
#
# `examples/gla.braid` is the other half of this story and is about
# VECTORS: a vector is a bundle, `Floatⁿ` bare wires, and the N-family
# words act on it.  This file is about MORPHISMS.  A matrix here is not
# an array and not a bundle; it is a WORD OF A CATEGORY, and the
# category is presented the way Bonchi, Sobociński and Zanasi present
# it (READING, "Matrices as a PROP"): matrices over a semiring are
# generated by a COMONOID (copy, discard), a MONOID (add, zero), the
# SYMMETRY, and the SCALARS — and transposition is the EXCHANGE OF THE
# COMONOID AND THE MONOID.  Every one of those is a slot below.
#
# ---- the guarantee is that `embed` is PARTIAL (2026-09-21) ----------
# The Doctrine's `embed : Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ ⇒ k(a, b)` takes ANY base function,
# so a theory that declares it makes `embed [fsin]` a linear map.  That
# assertion — an identity-on-objects functor from the WHOLE base — is
# simply false for linear maps.  What is true is a WIDE SUBCATEGORY
# `B_lin ⊆ B` and a functor `B_lin → K`, so `embed` is PARTIAL, and the
# partiality IS the guarantee.
#
# A wide subcategory is determined by a set of GENERATORS closed under
# composition, and the base is PRESENTED — so "is this program in the
# subcategory" reduces to "is every atom in the generator set", which
# is a syntactic scan.  `examples/transpose.braid`'s `linear?` runs
# exactly that scan at RUNTIME and answers with a Bool.  Here the
# generators are written beside the slots that name them —
#
#     copy : • ⇒ k(Float, Float Float) = dup
#
# — the scan happens at ELABORATION, and the answer is a REFUSAL:
#
#   `with Dense`: `fmul` has no image under GLA: its generators are
#   `dup`, `fadd`, `fzero`, `drop`, `swap`, `_`, and `fmul` is not one
#   of them, so the functor has nothing to send it to.
#
# So this file has both halves.  `in Dense` builds morphisms BY HAND
# out of the theory's own slots, which is the compose-only row of §8's
# table; and `with Dense` transports a base program, one ATOM at a
# time, when every atom is a generator.  Neither can name a nonlinear
# map, and the second is SOUND BUT INCOMPLETE: it rejects programs that
# are linear but spelled with an atom off the table, and never accepts
# one that is not linear.  That is the direction that matters.
#
# THE HONEST CAVEAT, and it is the one `examples/circuits.braid` ends
# on: `Mat` is an ordinary data constructor and Braid has no export
# lists, so `[fsin] [fsin] c ; Mat` builds a carrier from outside the
# vocabulary.  A model seals the CATEGORY — nothing written in GLA's
# words is nonlinear — and sealing the TYPE is a separate, unshipped
# question.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The carrier
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# STACK-SHAPED, not width-indexed.  The 2026-09-18 spec for this file
# wanted `data Mat(a, b) = Fn⟨Float^a ⇒ Float^b⟩` with `a` and `b`
# WIDTHS, and that shape is wrong twice over: `data` takes no width
# parameters and a constructor argument cannot be width-kinded, and —
# the deeper reason — the PROP's generators are tiny (copy 1→2, add
# 2→1, zero 0→1, discard 1→0) and everything else is built by
# composition AND TENSOR, so a width-indexed hom-object forces
# `k(a, b) k(c, d) ⇒ k(a+c, b+d)`: two variables ADDED, the deferred
# level-2 arithmetic.  Over STACKS the tensor is CONCATENATION, which
# is free because stacks are flat — `k(ρ, σ)` already names a whole
# side of a diagram.
#
# THREE FIELDS, and the middle one is the finding.  The spec said the
# `Code` is what makes `tr` possible: transposing a bare function means
# probing it at basis vectors, which is an anamorphism, so `tr` was to
# dualize the Code and REBUILD the function.  The rebuilding does not
# type.  `evalAs` is the only road from `Code` back to `Fn` and it
# needs a WITNESS at the arrow the code must meet — for `tr : k(a, b) ⇒
# k(b, a)` that witness is an `Fn⟨b ⇒ a⟩`, which is precisely the thing
# being built.  Written out, the model is refused at the slot:
#
#   model Dense: slot 'tr' is Mat(•, List(a0)) ⇒ Mat(•, List(a0)) but
#   theory GLA declares Mat(ρ0, ρ1) ⇒ Mat(ρ1, ρ0)
#
# So what `tr` needs is not the Code but the DUAL FUNCTION, carried
# beside the function: a linear map here is a map WITH A CHOSEN
# ADJOINT, which is what Elliott's linear-map representation is too
# (READING, the AD entry).  `tr` is then a projection — it swaps the
# two halves — and `tr ; tr = id` and contravariance hold by
# construction rather than by computation.
#
# The `Code` stays, for a different job it does better: it is the
# DIAGRAM, and `sameCodeC` decides equations on it.  Without it the
# transposition laws below could only be sampled; with it they are
# PROVED.
data Mat(a..., b...) = Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ Fn⟨b ⇒ a⟩ Code

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The theory
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `in Doctrine` is what gives the theory a CARRIER, and it is not
# optional: without it `transportOf` hands back no transport and
# `def m in Dense = …` is refused with *`in Dense` names a model of GLA
# in the base: it has no carrier to build*.  The claim checked is that
# `compose` is the Doctrine's `compose` at the Doctrine's signature,
# and it is.
#
# `under` is the theory's OWN strength, for the reason
# `examples/prob.braid` declares one: a base stage of any width embeds
# as itself, but a GENERATOR is not a base stage — `copy` is a carrier
# at the fixed width `k(Float, Float Float)`, and nothing in the base
# can widen it.  One wire rides UNDER.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, and cannot be: the monoidal product `m ⊗ n`.  A
# stack variable may only sit in tail position, so `k(c a, c b)` is
# spellable and `k(a c, b c)` is refused — *The stack parameter 'a'
# must be the last thing in its stack*.  The stack-shaped hom-object
# writes exactly ONE of the two whiskerings, and the symmetry at
# general stacks (`k(a c, c a)`) is unwritable for the same reason.
# `sym` therefore ships at the one concrete width that generates the
# rest in a PROP, and `m ⊗ id` — a generator with wires riding ABOVE —
# is out of reach.  The consequence is stated where it bites: the
# bialgebra law proper needs `copy ⊗ copy`, so the laws below are its
# unit, counit and scalar corollaries and not the four-wire equation
# itself.
#
# `app` and `diagram` are EXITS — carrier in, base out — read off their
# declared arrows.  `app` is what makes a matrix READ LIKE A FUNCTION:
# it hands back the real `Fn`, and `ev` runs it.
#
# THE `= <word>` CLAUSE is the generator's spelling in the BASE, and it
# is what `with Dense` reads backwards (§7).  It goes on the THEORY and
# not on a model, and that is the design decision: which base programs
# are linear is a property of the PRESENTATION, and two models of one
# theory must not be able to disagree about it.  Put the table on a
# reference model instead — `with Dense via Funcs` — and `B_lin` would
# depend on which model you named; and the inverse asked for would be
# the inverse of a model whose slot bodies are PROGRAMS, which is not a
# table at all.  Beside the signature, where the generator is.
#
# `scale` gets NO spelling, and the refusal that enforces it is written
# out in the theory checker: a base spelling is only for a slot that
# builds a carrier out of nothing or whiskers one, and `scale` takes a
# `Float`, so it is one generator PER VALUE and no single word names
# it.  That is the AFFINE TRAP pinned at the declaration: no literal can
# enter the table, so `x ; 1.0 ; fadd` — an affine map, not a linear one
# — has nothing to be read as.
theory GLA(k(..., ...)) in Doctrine =
    compose : k(a, b) k(b, c) ⇒ k(a, c)
    copy    : • ⇒ k(Float, Float Float)       = dup
    add     : • ⇒ k(Float Float, Float)       = fadd
    zero    : • ⇒ k(•, Float)                 = fzero
    discard : • ⇒ k(Float, •)                 = drop
    sym     : • ⇒ k(Float Float, Float Float) = swap
    under   : k(a, b) ⇒ k(c a, c b)           = _
    scale   : Float ⇒ k(Float, Float)
    tr      : k(a, b) ⇒ k(b, a)
    app     : k(a, b) ⇒ Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩
    diagram : k(a, b) ⇒ Code
    ## PROVED, by `sameCodeC` on the diagrams: transposition is an
    ## involution, it is contravariant, and it EXCHANGES the comonoid
    ## and the monoid.  These are the equations the presentation is
    ## about, and they are decided rather than sampled.
    law trInvolution = ((copy add ; compose) ; tr ; tr ; diagram) ((copy add ; compose) ; diagram) ; sameCodeC
    law trContravariant = ((copy add ; compose) ; tr ; diagram) (((add ; tr) (copy ; tr) ; compose) ; diagram) ; sameCodeC
    law trExchange = (copy ; tr ; diagram) (add ; diagram) ; sameCodeC
    law trUnitExchange = (zero ; tr ; diagram) (discard ; diagram) ; sameCodeC
    ## SAMPLED, through `app`: these relate two DIFFERENT diagrams that
    ## denote the same matrix, which is what a bialgebra law is, and no
    ## normalizer decides that — `copy ; add` normalizes to `dup >>
    ## fadd` and `2.0 ; scale` to a closure over a literal.
    law bialgebraDouble = ((copy add ; compose) ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev) ((2.0 ; scale) ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev) ; equals
    law comonoidCounit = ((copy (discard ; under) ; compose) ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev) 3.0 ; equals
    law monoidUnit = (((zero ; under) add ; compose) ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev) 3.0 ; equals
    law scalarSelfDual = ((2.0 ; scale ; tr) ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev) 6.0 ; equals

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. The model
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ELEMENT TYPE IS `Float` OUTRIGHT, not a carrier parameter.  A
# parameter would be the more general theory, and it is unwritable for
# the reason `examples/theories.braid` states at the top: a law over a
# parametric theory cannot invent a value of the parameter, and every
# law below SAMPLES at a literal.  A parametric GLA would need a
# `sample : • ⇒ e` slot and its generators would still be at one wire
# each; the generality bought would be a renaming.  One semiring, said
# once.
#
# `fzero` is the base word for the monoid's unit.  It exists so that
# the DIAGRAM's atom for `zero` is a WORD and not a literal — `0.0`
# read as an atom is a `Sym` like any other word (Code has no Float
# alternative), and a generator that dualizes to `drop` wants a name
# that dualizes back.
def orNil = ((c -> c) | drop ; nil) ; merge
def fzero = 0.0

## the atom-level dual: the comonoid's generators and the monoid's,
## exchanged.  `swap`, `_` and a scalar are self-dual and fall through.
def dualSym = (s -> (s .dup ; equals) [.fadd] [(s .fadd ; equals) [.dup] [(s .fzero ; equals) [.drop] [(s .drop ; equals) [.fzero] [s] ... ; cond] ... ; cond] ... ; cond] ... ; cond)
def dualAtom = [(s -> s ; dualSym ; alt1 ; Atom)] [(n -> n ; alt2 ; Atom)] [(t -> t ; alt3 ; Atom)] [(y -> y ; alt4 ; Atom)] [(c -> c ; alt5 ; Atom)] [(l b -> l b ; alt6 ; Atom)] [(c -> c ; alt7 ; Atom)] ... ; foldAtom

## THE TRANSPOSE OF A DIAGRAM: reverse the spine, dualize every atom.
## Reversing is contravariance; dualizing is the comonoid/monoid
## exchange.  Atoms WITHIN a stage are not reversed, because `f ⊗ g`
## transposes to `fᵀ ⊗ gᵀ` with the order kept.  This is
## `examples/transpose.braid`'s `transposeC`, at Float and with the
## monoid's unit added — and here it is a SLOT BODY with laws over it
## rather than a runtime fold nobody checks.
def dualC = reverse ; [[dualAtom] ... ; map] ... ; map

## a one-atom diagram from the word that names the generator
def wordC = (s -> s ; symStr ; parse ; orNil)

## whiskering a diagram: one wire rides under, so every stage gains a
## leading `_`
def padC = [(st -> (._ ; alt1 ; Atom) st ; cons)] ... ; map

## composition of two witnesses, at any width — `examples/reified.braid`
## writes the same four atoms, and `ev` is what makes it possible at all
def thenFn = (f g -> [f ... ; ev ; g ... ; ev])

def composeM =
    m n ->
    m ; unMat ; (f f' p -> n ; unMat ; (g g' q ->
        (f g ; thenFn) (g' f' ; thenFn) (p q ; append) ; Mat))
#   the dual composes the other way round — contravariance, written
#   into the slot body rather than checked after the fact

def copyM    = [dup] [fadd] (.dup ; wordC) ; Mat
def addM     = [fadd] [dup] (.fadd ; wordC) ; Mat
def zeroM    = [fzero] [drop] (.fzero ; wordC) ; Mat
def discardM = [drop] [fzero] (.drop ; wordC) ; Mat
def symM     = [swap] [swap] (.swap ; wordC) ; Mat

## A SCALAR HAS NO ONE-STAGE BASE SPELLING, and that is the affine trap
## in miniature.  `_ 2.0 ; fmul` is TWO stages — a constant (`• ⇒
## Float`, which is not linear) and a multiplication (`Float Float ⇒
## Float`, which is not linear in both arguments) — and reversing two
## stages does not transpose them.  So the scalar's diagram is one
## stage that NAMES the generator and carries its value, `scale 2.0`,
## and the price is paid in the open: `sameCodeC` refuses any diagram
## containing it (*`scale` has no closed arity*), which is why every
## scalar law above samples.
def scaleM = (s -> [(x -> x s ; fmul)] [(x -> x s ; fmul)] (("scale " (s ; toStr) ; cat) ; parse ; orNil) ; Mat)

def underM = (m -> m ; unMat ; (f g c -> [_ (f ... ; ev)] [_ (g ... ; ev)] (c ; padC) ; Mat))
def trM    = (m -> m ; unMat ; (f g c -> g f (c ; dualC) ; Mat))
def appM     = Mat(f, g, c) -> f
def diagramM = Mat(f, g, c) -> c

model Dense in GLA(Mat) =
    compose = composeM
    copy    = copyM
    add     = addM
    zero    = zeroM
    discard = discardM
    scale   = scaleM
    sym     = symM
    under   = underM
    tr      = trM
    app     = appM
    diagram = diagramM

# The eleven slots are checked against the theory and the eight laws
# above RUN — four decided by `sameCodeC`, four sampled through `app` —
# before anything below prints.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Morphisms, built by hand
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `in Dense` opens the vocabulary and applies nothing.  Each def below
# builds ONE carrier out of nothing, `• ⇒ Mat(a, b)`, which is what a
# word of the category is; the header is the written claim and the
# elaborator checks it against the arrow.  No receipt is minted,
# because `in` applied nothing — the type prints unfolded.

## x ↦ x + x.  Δ then ∇, the diagram the bialgebra law is about.
def dbl in Dense = copy add ; compose
#   dbl : • ⇒ Mat(Float, Float)

## the same matrix as a scalar
def two in Dense = 2.0 ; scale

## Δ then discard the copy: the comonoid's counit law, as a word
def cu in Dense = copy (discard ; under) ; compose

## ∇ with a zero pushed under: the monoid's unit law, as a word
def uz in Dense = (zero ; under) add ; compose

## the 1×2 matrix [1 1] and its transpose, the 2×1 column
def row11 in Dense = add
def col11 in Dense = add ; tr

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. A matrix READS LIKE A FUNCTION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `app` hands back the `Fn` the carrier holds and `ev` runs it, so the
# result is a first-class function value and not a second notation.
# Each def below names `app`, which is what makes the `in Dense` header
# mean something: a def that named none of the model's words and built
# no carrier would be refused for having done nothing.

"-- matrices applied --" ; print
def r1 in Dense = dbl ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev
r1 ; print                          # 6.0  — 3 + 3
def r2 in Dense = two ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev
r2 ; print                          # 6.0  — the same matrix, spelled as a scalar
def r3 in Dense = cu ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev
r3 ; print                          # 3.0  — copy, then throw one copy away
def r4 in Dense = uz ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev
r4 ; print                          # 3.0  — add zero
def r5 in Dense = row11 ; app ... ; _ 4.0 5.0 ; ev
r5 ; print                          # 9.0  — [1 1] · (4, 5)

# the 2×1 column applied: one in, two out.  A stage must cover the
# stack it is handed, so printing two wires is `_ print ; print` — the
# top one first, then the one that was riding below it.
def r6 in Dense = col11 ; app ... ; _ 7.0 ; ev
r6 ; _ print ; print                # 7.0 then 7.0 — the column (1, 1) at 7

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. ...and READS LIKE A DIAGRAM
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
"" ; print
"-- diagrams --" ; print
def d1 in Dense = dbl ; diagram
d1 ; unparse ; print                # dup >> fadd
def d2 in Dense = dbl ; tr ; diagram
d2 ; unparse ; print                # dup >> fadd — x+x is symmetric
def d3 in Dense = copy ; diagram
d3 ; unparse ; print                # dup
def d4 in Dense = copy ; tr ; diagram
d4 ; unparse ; print                # fadd — THE EXCHANGE: Δᵀ = ∇
def d5 in Dense = zero ; tr ; diagram
d5 ; unparse ; print                # drop — and the unitᵀ = the counit
def d6 in Dense = uz ; diagram
d6 ; unparse ; print                # _ fzero >> fadd — the whiskering is a `_`
def d7 in Dense = two ; diagram
d7 ; unparse ; print                # scale 2.0 — one stage, naming the generator

# A composite that is NOT symmetric, so the transpose is visibly a
# different diagram: x ↦ (2x, 2x) transposes to (u, v) ↦ 2u + 2v.
def fan in Dense = two copy ; compose
def d8 in Dense = fan ; diagram
d8 ; unparse ; print                # scale 2.0 >> dup
def d9 in Dense = fan ; tr ; diagram
d9 ; unparse ; print                # fadd >> scale 2.0
def r7 in Dense = fan ; app ... ; _ 5.0 ; ev
r7 ; _ print ; print                # 10.0 then 10.0
def r8 in Dense = fan ; tr ; app ... ; _ 1.0 2.0 ; ev
r8 ; print                          # 6.0 — 2·(1 + 2)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. `;` AS COMPOSITION — `embed` on a subcategory
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything above composes with a WORD, `f g ; compose`, because a
# `with` scope needs an embedding and the embedding is the thing this
# theory refuses to declare totally.  The base spellings on the slots
# are what make it declarable PARTIALLY, and then `with Dense` is an
# ordinary transported scope: `;` is composition and a base program
# becomes a matrix.
#
# THE TRANSPORT IS ATOM-WISE, which is the one new thing.  Standard
# transport sends `f ; g` to `embed [f] embed [g] ; compose`, embedding
# each STAGE opaquely; this reads each ATOM into a slot, so `dup ; fadd`
# becomes `copy add ; compose` and every generator in the composite is
# one the theory named.  A stage is `_`… followed by exactly one
# generator — the `_`s are `under` and the generator is the carrier they
# ride beneath — because only one whiskering is spellable (§2), so two
# generators side by side is a TENSOR and is refused by name.

"" ; print
"-- transported: the same program, read as a matrix --" ; print

## written once, as an ordinary base program would be...
def dbl2 with Dense = dup ; fadd
#   dbl2 : Float =Dense> Float — a RECEIPT, because the scope changed
#   the code; `dbl` above carries none, because `in` applied nothing.

## ...and it composes in the category like any other word of it
def quad with Dense = dbl2 ; dbl2
def triple with Dense = dup ; _ dup ; _ fadd ; fadd

def t1 in Dense = dbl2 ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev
t1 ; print                          # 6.0
def t2 in Dense = quad ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev
t2 ; print                          # 12.0 — 3 ⇒ 6 ⇒ 12
def t3 in Dense = triple ; app ... ; _ 2.0 ; ev
t3 ; print                          # 6.0  — x + (x + x)

## a transported matrix transposes like a hand-built one
def t4 in Dense = triple ; tr ; app ... ; _ 5.0 ; ev
t4 ; print                          # 15.0 — [3]ᵀ = [3]
def t5 in Dense = triple ; tr ; diagram
t5 ; unparse ; print                # dup >> _ dup >> _ fadd >> fadd
#   x ↦ 3x is symmetric, so its diagram transposes to itself — the `_`s
#   are self-dual and the comonoid/monoid exchange puts the tree back.

## and a hand-built morphism composes with a transported one inside the
## same scope, because `in Dense` put it in the same word table: `cu`
## is `copy (discard ; under) ; compose`, written by hand, and the
## scope leaves it alone instead of trying to read it as an atom
def mix with Dense = dbl2 ; cu ; dup ; fadd
def t6 in Dense = mix ; app ... ; _ 3.0 ; ev
t6 ; print                          # 12.0 — 3 ⇒ 6 ⇒ 6 ⇒ 12

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. What this file does not claim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# SOUND, NOT COMPLETE.  `with Dense` accepts exactly the programs built
# from the atoms the theory named, every one of which is linear; it
# rejects programs that are linear but spelled another way, and it never
# accepts one that is not.  Three refusals show the edges, and none of
# them can run in a file that must pass:
#
#   def nonlinear with Dense = dup ; fmul
#     `fmul` has no image under GLA: its generators are `dup`, `fadd`,
#     `fzero`, `drop`, `swap`, `_`, and `fmul` is not one of them, so
#     the functor has nothing to send it to.
#
#   def affine with Dense = dup ; _ 1.0 ; fadd
#     `1.0` has no image under GLA: a LITERAL is not a generator of it.
#     A literal is a stage `• ⇒ τ`, and a constant introduced into a
#     diagram is what makes a map AFFINE rather than linear.
#
#   def tensored with Dense = dup dup
#     the stage `dup dup` puts two of GLA's generators side by side,
#     which is their TENSOR — and a hom-object over stacks writes only
#     one of the two whiskerings, so GLA has nothing to send it to.
#
# The middle one is the AFFINE TRAP, and it is worth seeing why it is
# not a special case in the elaborator: the theory checker refuses a
# base spelling on any slot that is not a generator, `scale` is such a
# slot, so no literal is ever in the table, so a literal is refused by
# the ordinary "no image" rule with a message that says which trap it
# is.
#
# WHAT IS STILL NOT REACHABLE.  A transported program cannot scale,
# because a scalar has no one-word base spelling (§3); `two`, `fan` and
# every scalar law above are hand-built for that reason.  And `m ⊗ n`
# is not constructible at all, in either half, because only one
# whiskering is spellable — which is why the bialgebra law proper is
# absent and its corollaries are what the theory states.
#
# AND `examples/transpose.braid` STAYS.  Its `linear?` asks this file's
# question of code that arrives at RUN TIME — from `parse`, from
# `evalCode` — and no scope can be put around a string read from a
# file.  One spelling for the static question, which is this file; one
# for the dynamic one, which is that one.
